---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Recording from '../components/Recording.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith('/') ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const repo = 'https://github.com/y0c0ms/sesync';
const docs = `${repo}/blob/main/docs`;
---
<Layout title="sesync: one conversation across coding agent harnesses" description="sesync keeps one AI coding conversation in step across omp, Pi, OpenCode, Claude Code and Antigravity CLI, with a lock, a TUI, a herdr popup, a phone bridge and machine-to-machine prompts.">

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <span class="kicker"><b>sesync</b> v0.1.0 · MIT · Linux and Windows builds</span>
      <h1>One conversation, every harness.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Start a conversation in omp, continue it in Pi, pick it up in OpenCode, resume it in Claude Code or Antigravity. sesync converts each copy, keeps them in step, and locks the conversation while a turn is running.</p>
      <p>A TUI on the machine, a popup inside herdr, a bridge for the phone, and prompts that travel from one machine to another.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${repo}/releases`}>Download v0.1.0</a>
        <a class="button" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
        <a class="button" href="#herdr">See the herdr popup</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-media">
      <Recording name="popup-herdr" alt="The sesync TUI opening as a herdr popup" width={1260} height={700}
        caption="The TUI as a herdr popup: <kbd>ctrl+alt+s</kbd>, pick a session, <kbd>o</kbd> opens it in a tab." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="what">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>A conversation has one <strong>source</strong>: the harness where it is live. Every other harness you chose gets a converted <strong>mirror</strong>. Answering inside a mirror moves the source there. The other mirrors then follow.</p>

    <div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="The source conversation is converted into mirrors in each chosen harness">
      <div class="node source"><strong>Source</strong><br />the live harness<br />(for example omp)</div>
      <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="mirrors">
        <div class="node">Mirror: Pi</div>
        <div class="node">Mirror: OpenCode</div>
        <div class="node">Mirror: Claude Code</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Turn-taking.</strong> A reply typed into a mirror makes that harness the source.</li>
      <li><strong>The lock.</strong> A conversation is locked while a harness takes a turn. The lock is claimed at turn start and released at turn end. An idle conversation holds nothing. A refused claim blocks the prompt and names the holder.</li>
      <li><strong>Mirrors are derived.</strong> A mirror is rebuilt from its source. The daemon skips any conversation that is locked.</li>
      <li><strong>Archive before replace.</strong> Bytes that sesync did not write are archived before anything replaces them.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The format converter is <code>sesync-conv</code>. The omp/Pi journal is the interchange format. Reasoning content is kept for omp, Pi, OpenCode and Antigravity, and dropped for Claude Code, which signs its own reasoning blocks.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tui">
    <h2>The TUI</h2>
    <p>Run <code>sesync --tui</code>. The tabs are <strong>Sessions</strong>, <strong>Team</strong> and <strong>Machines</strong>. The command palette is <kbd>ctrl+p</kbd>. The divergence dialog opens when two copies of a conversation have moved on separately. <strong>Open in herdr</strong> (<kbd>o</kbd>) opens the selected session in herdr.</p>

    <Recording name="tui-sessions" alt="Sessions tab" width={1260} height={700} caption="Each conversation with its mirrors, the age of the last good sync, and its team state. The details pane lists each copy and the last messages." steps={[`The list, then the details pane on a wide terminal`, `Share a session: the picker, then <code>a</code> for every harness`, `The filter cycles: all, marked, mirrored, failing`]} />

    <Recording name="tui-team" alt="Team tab" width={1260} height={700} caption="The sessions this machine shares and the ones the team holds, with where each stands. <kbd>i</kbd> imports a session the team has into a local harness." steps={[`Each row: in step, waiting to push, behind, or diverged`, `Import the copy another machine shared`]} />

    <Recording name="tui-machines" alt="Machines tab" width={1260} height={700} caption="Paired machines, their address and status, and the conversations each reports. <kbd>enter</kbd> attaches to the selected conversation in a herdr tab." steps={[`Tab to Machines, pick a conversation on the other machine`, `<kbd>enter</kbd>: its pane opens live in herdr`, `Type into that agent from here`]} />

    <Recording name="tui-palette" alt="Command palette" width={1260} height={700} caption="Every command for the current tab, searchable, including team and colour-scheme commands." steps={[`<kbd>ctrl+p</kbd>, then a few letters`, `Run the command with <kbd>enter</kbd>`]} />

    <h3>Divergence dialog</h3>
    <p>When two copies of a conversation each have new turns after their common history, the dialog shows both sides. It offers five choices and "decide later". Every choice except "decide later" keeps both histories.</p>
    <p>Full details: <a href={`${docs}/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="herdr">
    <h2>The herdr popup</h2>
    <p>herdr is a terminal multiplexer for coding agents. Add this to <code>~/.config/herdr/config.toml</code>, with <code>sesync</code> on herdr's PATH:</p>
<pre><code>[[keys.command]]
key = "ctrl+alt+s"
type = "popup"
command = "sesync --tui"
description = "sesync: open a session"
width = "90%"
height = "85%"</code></pre>
    <p>Press <kbd>ctrl+alt+s</kbd> in herdr to open the TUI as a popup. Pick a session and choose <strong>Open in herdr</strong>. If a herdr pane already runs that session, its tab is focused. Otherwise a new tab opens in the session's directory and types the harness's resume command.</p>
    <Recording name="popup-herdr" alt="The sesync popup inside herdr" width={1260} height={700} caption="herdr, then the TUI as a popup on <kbd>ctrl+alt+s</kbd>, then <kbd>o</kbd>: the session opens in a new herdr tab and the popup closes." steps={[`<kbd>ctrl+alt+s</kbd> over any pane: the TUI appears in a popup`, `Pick a session; <kbd>o</kbd> opens it in herdr`, `Press it again from another workspace`]} />
  </section>

  <section id="phone">
    <h2>Phone app control</h2>
    <p>The Agent Hub app for Android runs the bridge's protocol. It is built on Orca's mobile protocol. The bridge is <code>sesync bridge serve</code>. It listens on <code>0.0.0.0:6768</code> by default.</p>

    <h3>Pairing</h3>
    <pre><code>sesync bridge pair --endpoint ws://HOST:6768 --label phone</code></pre>
    <p>This prints a pairing offer, an <code>agenthub://pair?code=</code> URL and a terminal QR code. The device token appears only in that output. <code>--endpoint</code> is the address the phone dials. If you omit it, the <code>endpoint</code> value from the settings file is used. If neither is set, pairing stops and asks for one. Nothing here opens a firewall port.</p>
    <p>Show the QR code only to the phone being paired. Whoever can read the offer can pair. Remove a device with <code>sesync bridge revoke &lt;device-id&gt;</code>. Its token is refused on the next frame.</p>

    <h3>What the phone can do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>List conversations, with their project and herdr status.</li>
      <li>Read each transcript, and open its herdr pane live.</li>
      <li>Send prompts and answer approvals.</li>
      <li>Send images, and send or receive files.</li>
      <li>Dictate prompts (needs a Whisper-compatible server).</li>
      <li>Start new sessions.</li>
      <li>Receive push notifications through ntfy, when ntfy is configured.</li>
      <li>Show plan usage, read from <code>omp usage --json</code>.</li>
      <li>Set the <strong>"can see"</strong> switch. It links one paired machine to another, so each machine can reach the other.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="phone-row">
      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}media/app-chat.png`} alt="Agent Hub chat view with a prompt and a reply" width="1080" height="2400" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Chat view: prompt, reply and an image.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}media/app-approval.png`} alt="Agent Hub approval card" width="1080" height="2400" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Approval: a tool call waiting for a decision.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}media/app-resume.png`} alt="Agent Hub conversation resumed on the phone" width="1080" height="2400" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Resume: the same conversation, continued from the phone.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <h3>Encryption</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Machine links</strong> (<code>sesync machine</code>) use a hybrid handshake: X25519 + ML-KEM-768, with AES-256-GCM frames. A bridge that answers only with the legacy channel is refused.</li>
      <li><strong>Phone link</strong> uses Orca's legacy channel: X25519 + XSalsa20-Poly1305. This is classical, not hybrid. The bridge's docs say it is acceptable only because it runs inside WireGuard, which is also classical, until the app speaks the hybrid hello.</li>
      <li>The bridge is authenticated by its pinned X25519 key. Tokens are 256 random bits, and only their SHA-256 is stored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Settings file</h3>
    <p><code>~/.config/sesync/bridge/settings.json</code> holds this machine's values. All keys are optional. A flag wins over the file. An unknown field or invalid JSON stops the bridge and names the file.</p>
<pre><code>&#123; "endpoint": "ws://HOST:6768",
  "allow": "127.0.0.0/8,10.0.0.0/8",
  "ntfyUrl": "https://NTFY_HOST", "ntfyTopic": "agents", "ntfyTokenCommand": "cat ~/.config/ntfy-token",
  "whisperUrl": "http://WHISPER_HOST:8080/v1/audio/transcriptions",
  "whisperModel": "MODEL", "whisperLanguage": "en" &#125;</code></pre>
    <p>The default allowlist is loopback, the private ranges and the shared VPN range (<code>100.64.0.0/10</code>). Without ntfy settings, push is off.</p>
    <p>Full details: <a href={`${docs}/BRIDGE.md`}>docs/BRIDGE.md</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="machines">
    <h2>Machines</h2>
    <p>One sesync machine can drive another's conversations. Pair them with <code>sesync bridge pair --label &lt;this machine&gt;</code> on the other machine, then add it here.</p>
<pre><code>sesync machine add &lt;name&gt; &lt;pairing link&gt;
sesync machine list [&lt;name&gt;]
sesync machine attach &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation title or id&gt; [--open]
sesync machine prompt &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation&gt; &lt;text | -&gt; [--open] [--wait[=&lt;seconds&gt;]]
sesync machine read &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation&gt; [&lt;count&gt;]
sesync machine send &lt;name&gt; &lt;file or folder&gt;
sesync machine get &lt;name&gt; &lt;path on that machine&gt; [&lt;folder here&gt;]
sesync machine endpoint &lt;name&gt; &lt;ws://address&gt; | --clear
sesync machine remove &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>

    <Recording name="machine-prompt" alt="A prompt sent to a conversation on another machine" width={1260} height={620} caption="<code>sesync machine prompt --open --wait</code>: a closed conversation on the other machine is resumed, the prompt is sent as a turn, and its answer comes back." steps={[`<code>sesync machine list laptop</code>: what runs there`, `<code>prompt</code> with <code>--open --wait</code>`, `<code>read</code>: the last messages`]} />

    <h3>Agents prompting agents</h3>
    <p>An agent can use the <code>prompt-agent-over-bridge</code> skill from <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/skills`}>skills/</a> to ask an agent on another machine. <code>prompt</code> claims the conversation's lock for the turn. It refuses a pane that is waiting on a question, and a closed conversation unless <code>--open</code> is given.</p>
    <p>A paired phone can type into your agents. Treat each paired machine the same way.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="flags">
    <h2>Flags and commands</h2>
    <p>Taken from the usage strings in the source. <code>sesync</code> exits with code 2 on a usage error.</p>

    <h3><code>sesync</code> (<code>sesync/src/bin/sesync.rs</code>)</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>sesync --once</code></td><td>Run one sync pass and exit. Prints <code>one pass done, N source(s) changed</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync --watch</code></td><td>Run continuously. Poll interval from <code>SESYNC_POLL</code> (seconds, default 5). Only one daemon per state directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync --status</code></td><td>Print the mirror view.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync --tui</code></td><td>Open the manage TUI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync mirrors [--apply]</code></td><td>List mirror copies that can be removed. A dry run by default. <code>--apply</code> removes them; stop the daemon first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team create &lt;name&gt; [--member &lt;m&gt;]...</code></td><td>Create a team (admin). Needs <code>SESYNC_TEAM_URL</code> and <code>SESYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN_FILE</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team invite &lt;name&gt; &lt;member&gt;</code></td><td>Invite a member.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team roster &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Show the team's listed and pending machines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team approve &lt;name&gt; &lt;member&gt; &lt;machine&gt; [--fingerprint &lt;fp&gt;]</code></td><td>Approve a join request. Compare the fingerprint with the member first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team decline &lt;name&gt; &lt;member&gt; &lt;machine&gt;</code></td><td>Decline a join request.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team revoke &lt;name&gt; &lt;member&gt; &lt;machine&gt;</code></td><td>Revoke a machine.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team remove &lt;name&gt; &lt;member&gt;</code></td><td>Remove a member.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team join &lt;name&gt; --member &lt;m&gt;</code></td><td>Join a team as this machine. Asks for the 6-digit code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync team renew &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Renew this machine's team token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync herdr check [--herdr &lt;exe&gt;]</code></td><td>Check what sesync needs from herdr, in a throwaway herdr session. <code>--herdr</code> defaults to <code>HERDR_BIN_PATH</code>, else <code>herdr</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync herdr guard [--remove]</code></td><td>Write, or with <code>--remove</code> delete, herdr's <code>update-guard.toml</code>. herdr updates then warn and can run <code>sesync herdr check</code> first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync machine ...</code></td><td>Machine pairing and prompts. See <a href="#machines">Machines</a>. Source: <code>sesync/src/machines/cli.rs</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sesync bridge ...</code></td><td>Phone and machine bridge. See the table below. Source: <code>sesync/src/bridge/cli.rs</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3><code>sesync machine</code> (<code>sesync/src/machines/cli.rs</code>)</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>add &lt;name&gt; &lt;pairing link&gt;</code></td><td>Add a machine from <code>sesync bridge pair --label</code> output on that machine.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>list [&lt;name&gt;]</code></td><td>List machines, or one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>attach &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation&gt; [--open]</code></td><td>Mirror the conversation's pane here and type into it. <kbd>Ctrl-]</kbd> detaches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>prompt &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation&gt; &lt;text | -&gt; [--open] [--wait[=&lt;seconds&gt;]]</code></td><td>Send a prompt turn. <code>-</code> reads stdin. <code>--open</code> resumes a closed conversation. <code>--wait</code> prints the answer (default 600 s).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>read &lt;name&gt; &lt;conversation&gt; [&lt;count&gt;]</code></td><td>Print the last messages (default 10).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>send &lt;name&gt; &lt;file or folder&gt;</code></td><td>Upload into that machine's inbox. A folder is sent as a zip.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get &lt;name&gt; &lt;path&gt; [&lt;folder here&gt;]</code></td><td>Download a file, or a folder as a zip.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>endpoint &lt;name&gt; &lt;ws://address&gt; | --clear</code></td><td>Dial that machine at this address instead of the paired one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>remove &lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Forget the machine here.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3><code>sesync bridge</code> (<code>sesync/src/bridge/cli.rs</code>)</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>pair [--endpoint ws://HOST:PORT] [--label NAME]</code></td><td>Pair a new device. Prints the offer, the URL and a QR code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>devices</code></td><td>List paired devices. Never prints tokens.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>revoke &lt;device-id&gt;</code></td><td>Remove a device.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>serve [--listen ADDR:PORT] [--allow CIDR,...] [--herdr-socket PATH] [--ntfy-url URL] [--ntfy-topic NAME] [--ntfy-token-command CMD] [--no-ntfy] [--usage-every SECONDS] [--omp PATH] [--no-usage]</code></td><td>Serve in the foreground until Ctrl-C. Defaults: <code>0.0.0.0:6768</code>, plan usage every 600 s.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3><code>sesync-lock</code> (<code>sesync-lock/src/main.rs</code>)</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>claim &lt;id&gt; &lt;harness&gt; &lt;pid&gt; [target] [--force]</code></td><td>Claim a conversation's lock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>release &lt;id&gt; &lt;harness&gt; &lt;pid&gt;</code></td><td>Release it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>check &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Check the lock. Exit 2 means locked; it prints <code>harness pid</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>resolve &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Map a mirror id to its source id.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sweep | list</code></td><td>Clear stale locks, or list them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>hook &lt;event&gt; &lt;harness&gt; --pid &lt;pid&gt; [--session &lt;id&gt;]</code></td><td>Harness hook. Reads a JSON payload on stdin. Events: <code>agent_end</code>, <code>message_end</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Exit codes: 0 ok or free, 2 locked, 1 error.</p>

    <h3><code>sesync-conv</code> (<code>sesync-conv/src/main.rs</code>)</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>inspect &lt;journal&gt;</code></td><td>Summarise a journal: roles and block types.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-pi &lt;journal&gt; --out-dir &lt;dir&gt; [--id UUID] [--title T]</code></td><td>Write a Pi session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-omp &lt;journal&gt; --out-dir &lt;dir&gt; --home &lt;HOME&gt; [--id UUID] [--title T]</code></td><td>Write an omp session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-opencode &lt;journal&gt; [--out bundle.json] --id SES_ID --title T [--cli-version V] [--no-thinking] [--context-window N] [--context-share F] [--context-overhead N]</code></td><td>Write an OpenCode bundle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-claude &lt;journal&gt; --out-dir &lt;~/.claude/projects&gt; [--id UUID] [--title T] [--cli-version V] [--context-window N] [--context-share F] [--context-overhead N]</code></td><td>Write a Claude Code session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-antigravity &lt;journal&gt; --store &lt;dir&gt; --id &lt;id&gt; [--title T]</code></td><td>Write an Antigravity conversation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>from-claude &lt;transcript.jsonl&gt; --out &lt;journal.jsonl&gt;</code></td><td>Convert a Claude Code transcript to a journal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>from-opencode &lt;export.json&gt; --out &lt;journal.jsonl&gt;</code></td><td>Convert an OpenCode export to a journal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>from-antigravity ...</code></td><td>Convert an Antigravity conversation to a journal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>antigravity-retitle --store &lt;dir&gt; --id &lt;id&gt; --title T</code></td><td>Change an Antigravity conversation's title.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ids &lt;seed&gt;</code></td><td>Print the deterministic OpenCode and Claude ids.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>The <code>--context-*</code> flags fit the copy to the target's context window. See <a href={`${docs}/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</a>, "Context fit".</p>

    <h3><code>sesync bridge</code> and <code>sesync team</code> settings</h3>
    <p>Environment: <code>SESYNC_DIR</code> (state, default <code>~/.local/share/sesync</code>), <code>SESYNC_CONFIG_DIR</code>, <code>SESYNC_POLL</code>, <code>SESYNC_TEAM_URL</code>, <code>SESYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN_FILE</code>, <code>HERDR_BIN_PATH</code>. Full list: the Configuration table in the <a href={`${repo}#configuration`}>README</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="install">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <h3>From a release</h3>
    <p>Download an archive from <a href={`${repo}/releases`}>GitHub Releases</a>. Each release ships three binaries (<code>sesync-lock</code>, <code>sesync-conv</code>, <code>sesync</code>), the hook files and a <code>SHA256SUMS</code> file. The archives are tar.gz for Linux and macOS and zip for Windows. The binaries are not signed.</p>

    <h3>From source</h3>
    <p>You need Rust (stable). Build the three binaries, then copy them onto your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
<pre><code>cargo build --release --manifest-path sesync-lock/Cargo.toml
cargo build --release --manifest-path sesync-conv/Cargo.toml
cargo build --release --manifest-path sesync/Cargo.toml</code></pre>

    <h3><code>install.sh</code> (Linux and macOS)</h3>
    <p>Run <code>sh install.sh</code>. It builds the three binaries with <code>cargo build --release</code>, then installs them into <code>~/.local/bin</code>. The hook files go into <code>~/.local/share/sesync/hooks</code>. A <code>MANIFEST</code> lists every file it wrote. On Linux with systemd it writes <code>~/.config/systemd/user/sesync.service</code> and enables it, so the daemon starts at login. The other options:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--prefix DIR</code>: install under <code>DIR</code>.</li>
      <li><code>--no-service</code>: do not register the login service.</li>
      <li><code>--no-build</code>: use already-built release binaries.</li>
      <li><code>--uninstall</code>: stop the service and remove exactly the files in the manifest.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3><code>install.ps1</code> (Windows)</h3>
    <p>Run <code>powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1</code>. It needs no administrator rights. It writes only under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\sesync</code>, plus two per-user settings. It adds the <code>bin</code> folder to the user <code>PATH</code> (in <code>HKCU\Environment</code>), and adds a login start entry under <code>HKCU\...\Run</code>. The options are <code>-NoService</code> (skip the login entry) and <code>-Uninstall</code>.</p>

    <h3>Hooks per harness</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Harness</th><th>Hook</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>omp and Pi</td><td><code>hooks/omp-lock-shim.ts</code>, an omp extension. Claims on <code>turn_start</code>, releases on <code>turn_end</code> and session lifecycle events.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>hooks/claude-hook.sh</code>, wired to <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> (claim, block on refusal) and <code>Stop</code> / <code>SessionEnd</code> (release).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OpenCode</td><td>A plugin that claims on <code>chat.message</code> and releases on <code>session.idle</code> and <code>session.deleted</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Antigravity CLI (<code>agy</code>)</td><td>No hook yet. The daemon still claims the lock for every write, refuses to refresh a copy that agy holds open, and archives any agy turn it would replace.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3>herdr popup</h3>
    <p>See <a href="#herdr">The herdr popup</a>. <code>sesync herdr check</code> runs a throwaway herdr session to verify the setup.</p>
